.TH SLEEP 2
.SH NAME
sleep, alarm \- delay, ask for delayed note
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "sleep :: proc(ticks: u64) -> i64"
.B "alarm :: proc(ticks: u64) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sleep
suspends the calling process for
.I ticks
of the system clock.
A
.I ticks
of zero gives up the processor if another thread is ready to run, and
returns at once otherwise.
A note that arrives during the sleep cuts it short, and the answer is a
negative
.BR EINTR .
.PP
.I Alarm
asks for an
.B alarm
note (see
.IR notify (2))
to be posted to the caller after
.I ticks
of the clock.
A later call to
.I alarm
replaces the pending one; a
.I ticks
of zero cancels it.
The answer is how many ticks were left on the alarm that was pending
before the call, or zero if none was.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR notify (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers zero or more on success and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
